<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">18 July in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 18 July, from 477 BCE to 1936.</p></header>
<div class="tih-pinned-card"><p class="tih-pinned-label">International day</p><p class="tih-pinned-title">Nelson Mandela International Day</p><p class="tih-pinned-desc">Proclaimed by the United Nations in 2009 on the birthday of Nelson Mandela, this day invites individuals to devote 67 minutes of service to their communities — one minute for each year Mandela spent in public life.</p></div>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-republic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">477 BCE <span class="tih-ago">2,502 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Veii ambushes and destroys the Fabii at the Cremera</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Etruscan city of Veii ambushed the Roman Fabii, who had been raiding its territory, and killed them all at the Cremera. Only Quintus Fabius Vibulanus, too young to go to war, survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">64 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,962 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Great Fire of Rome breaks out</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Fire broke out near the Circus Maximus and raged for days, gutting ten of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s fourteen districts. Rumour blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> records that he blamed the Christians, beginning their first persecution.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1195">1195 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">831 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Almohads defeat Alfonso VIII of Castile at Alarcos</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">Abu Yusuf Yaqub al-Mansur</a> defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, whose army retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, while the Almohads retook Trujillo, Montánchez and Talavera. The battle marked the high point of Almohad power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1290">1290 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">736 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Edward I orders the expulsion of the Jews from England</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Edward I issued the Edict of Expulsion, ordering all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by 1 November. Several thousand were forced out, and Jews were not readmitted until the Cromwellian era of the 1650s.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1389">1389 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">637 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">England and France agree the Truce of Leulinghem</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Richard II's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Charles VI's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, both facing serious internal troubles, agreed the Truce of Leulinghem, ending the Caroline War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. It began a 13-year peace, the longest of the war.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1863">1863 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">163 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The 54th Massachusetts leads the assault on Fort Wagner</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The 54th Massachusetts, one of the first Black regiments in the Union army, led a night attack on Fort Wagner near Charleston. The assault failed with heavy losses, but the regiment's courage changed Northern opinion.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1870">1870 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">156 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The First Vatican Council defines papal infallibility</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The council adopted Pastor aeternus, declaring the Pope free from error when he defines doctrine on faith or morals ex cathedra. The decree strengthened papal authority and caused a schism with the Old Catholics.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1925">1925 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">101 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The first volume of Hitler's Mein Kampf is published</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Hitler's autobiography and manifesto, written partly in prison, set out his antisemitism and his aim of conquering 'living space' in the east. Its ideas foreshadowed the policies of the Third Reich.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1936">1936 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">90 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Military uprising launches the Spanish Civil War</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Following the army revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> the day before, garrisons across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> rose against the Republic. The war lasted nearly three years and ended in General Franco's dictatorship.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 18 July <span class="n">6</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/heinrich-bullinger.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Heinrich Bullinger</span><span class="tp">1504–1575</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/zacharias-ursinus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zacharias-ursinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Zacharias Ursinus</span><span class="tp">1534–1583</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-ii-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/rudolf-ii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="tp">1552–1612</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/vidkun-quisling"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Vidkun Quisling</span><span class="tp">1887–1945</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mohammad-daoud-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mohammad Daoud Khan</span><span class="tp">1909–1978</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nelson-mandela.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Nelson Mandela</span><span class="tp">1918–2013</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 18 July <span class="n">6</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/godfrey-of-bouillon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Godfrey of Bouillon</span><span class="tp">c. 1060–1100</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/caravaggio.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Caravaggio</span><span class="tp">1571–1610</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-of-saxe-weimar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bernhard-of-saxe-weimar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Bernhard of Saxe-Weimar</span><span class="tp">1604–1639</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/antoine-watteau"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/antoine-watteau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Antoine Watteau</span><span class="tp">1684–1721</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jane-austen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jane-austen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Jane Austen</span><span class="tp">1775–1817</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/benito-juarez.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Benito Juárez</span><span class="tp">1806–1872</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
